10:23:28 | INFO | 🤖 Target: A car is like a skateboard in the relevant physics. Wheels do not make the rule different. The key distinction is **what you are trying to land on**: - If you jump **inside a moving car**, you are trying to land back on the moving car floor. - If you jump **off a moving skateboard**, you usually land on the stationary ground. Before leaving the skateboard, you and it are both moving forward together. When you jump off, you retain that forward speed, so you do not drop straight down to the spot on the road where you jumped—you travel forward while falling. But the road is not moving with you, so you land farther ahead on the road. Meanwhile, the skateboard may continue forward too, perhaps at almost the same speed briefly, though rolling resistance, friction, and any change caused by your jump can alter its speed. If you could jump vertically above a skateboard moving steadily and then land **back on the skateboard**, you would tend to land close to the same place on it—just like in a car. In practice it is harder because: - a skateboard can wobble, slow, turn, or shoot out; - your jump can push it backward or forward; - there is no enclosing cabin to shield you from wind; - balancing is difficult. Likewise, if you jump *out of* a moving car, you will not land beside the car’s original position: you keep moving forward and hit the ground while carrying the car’s former forward speed. That is why jumping from a moving vehicle is dangerous. So: car, skateboard, bike, and airplane all obey the same inertia principle. The apparent difference comes from whether your landing surface moves with you, and how steadily the vehicle continues moving.
